.TH MUSICPLAYER 1 2026-10-26 musicplayer
.SH NAME
musicplayer \- minimal music player for the macOS terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B music
.br
.B artist
.PP
.B mpdir
.br
.B mpsub
.br
.B mpplay
.br
.B mpartist
.br
.B mpfind
.RI [ pattern ]
.br
.I command
.B | mppipe
.PP
.B mpadd
.br
.B mpplayl
.br
.B mpview
.br
.B mpclear
.PP
.B mpman
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B musicplayer
is a set of shell functions for bash and zsh, for browsing a music
collection and playing it from the command line.
Tracks are picked with
.BR fzf (1)
and played one after the other with
.BR afplay (1).
There is no library to import: the music directory is the library,
and the playlist is a plain text file.
.PP
To load the functions, add this line to
.I ~/.zshrc
(zsh) or
.I ~/.bash_profile
(bash), using the path where you put
.BR musicplayer :
.PP
.RS
.EX
source ~/musicplayer/musicplayer.sh
.EE
.RE
.PP
.BR music ,
.BR artist ,
.B mpartist
and
.B mpfind
change the current directory of your shell,
which is why
.B musicplayer
is a set of shell functions rather than a program.
.PP
Press
.B Ctrl\-C
to stop playback.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Browsing
.TP
.B music
Change to the music directory and list its contents with
.BR less (1).
Press
.B q
to quit.
.TP
.B artist
Pick an artist directory from the music directory, change to it and list its
contents.
Move with the arrow keys and press
.B Enter
to select.
The preview on the right shows what is in the highlighted directory.
.SS Playing
.TP
.B mpdir
Play all tracks in the current directory, in alphabetical order.
.TP
.B mpsub
Play all tracks in the current directory and all its subdirectories,
in random order: a new order each time.
To play them in alphabetical order instead, see
.BR EXAMPLES .
.TP
.B mpplay
Pick tracks in the current directory and play them.
Press
.B Tab
to select a track and go to the next one, or
.B Shift\-Tab
to select a track and go to the previous one, then press
.BR Enter .
.TP
.B mpartist
Pick an artist directory, as with
.BR artist ,
and play all tracks in it and its subdirectories in random order, as with
.BR mpsub .
.TP
\fBmpfind\fR [\fIpattern\fR]
Search the whole music directory for tracks whose file name contains
.IR pattern ,
pick one and play it.
The search is case-sensitive.
Without a
.IR pattern ,
all tracks are listed.
Leaves you in the music directory.
.TP
\fIcommand\fR | \fBmppipe\fR
Play the files listed by
.IR command ,
one path per line.
Relative paths are relative to the current directory.
.SS Playlist
There is one playlist: a text file with the full path of one track on each
line.
.TP
.B mpadd
Pick tracks in the current directory and its subdirectories and add them to
the end of the playlist.
Select several tracks with
.B Tab
and
.BR Shift\-Tab ,
as in
.BR mpplay .
.TP
.B mpplayl
Play the playlist from the top.
.TP
.B mpview
Open the playlist in your editor, to reorder or remove tracks.
.TP
.B mpclear
Clear the playlist.
.SS Help
.TP
.B mpman
Show this manual.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Set these before the line that sources
.IR musicplayer.sh .
.TP
.B MUSICPLAYER_MUSIC_DIR
The music directory.
The default is
.IR "~/Music/iTunes/iTunes Music" .
The Music app usually keeps its files in
.IR ~/Music/Music/Media.localized/Music .
.TP
.B MUSICPLAYER_PLAYLIST
The playlist file.
The default is
.IR ~/.mpplaylist.txt .
.TP
.B EDITOR
The editor used by
.BR mpview .
The default is
.BR vim (1).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.mpplaylist.txt
The playlist, unless
.B MUSICPLAYER_PLAYLIST
is set.
.SH EXAMPLES
Pick an artist, then play one of their albums:
.PP
.RS
.EX
artist
cd "Abbey Road"
mpdir
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find a track by name and play it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mpfind Blackbird
.EE
.RE
.PP
Play all tracks below the current directory in alphabetical order instead of
random order.
This is what
.B mpsub
runs, with
.B sort
in place of
.BR "sort \-R" ;
.B "mpsub | sort"
would not work, because
.B mpsub
plays the tracks itself:
.PP
.RS
.EX
find . \-type f \e( \-name "*.mp3" \-o \-name "*.m4a" \e) |
    sort | mppipe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Play the playlist in random order:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sort \-R ~/.mpplaylist.txt | mppipe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Play the last five tracks added to the playlist:
.PP
.RS
.EX
tail \-n 5 ~/.mpplaylist.txt | mppipe
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B mpdir
and
.B mpsub
only play files ending in
.I .mp3
or
.IR .m4a .
.B mppipe
plays any format that
.BR afplay (1)
can play.
.PP
.BR afplay (1)
comes with macOS, so
.B musicplayer
only runs on macOS.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR afplay (1),
.BR fzf (1),
.BR less (1)
